  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins
    
    The Midnight Claws emerged from the tunnel gangs that formed during the Faction Wars, when official authority in Nightfall Shelf collapsed and someone had to keep the fire-cores flowing. The Book of the Berg, Nightfall Shelf's creation scripture, names them 'the Order of the First Dark'—a sacred priesthood that kept fire-cores burning and tunnels sealed when legitimate governance meant nothing, *the keepers of acts essential to survival that no light should be permitted to see.* Whether this reflects historical memory or retroactive sanctification is a question the scripture does not invite.
    
    ## Operations and the Shadow Economy
    
    For approximately thirty winters, the Claws ruled Nightfall Shelf's shadow economy—smuggling, enforcement, and the quiet disappearances that the Fire Keepers officially denied knowledge of.
    
    Their core operation was fire-core diversion: eleven hundred units over thirty winters, per the tribunal ledger presented by Chief Fire Keeper Velumeth~ at the dissolution proceedings. The figure is suspiciously round; I note it without insisting on its falsehood. The smuggling operation created dependency by design. Fire-cores diverted from official channels ensured that settlements receiving Claws supply during shortages learned legitimate distribution could not be trusted, because the Claws had already bled it dry. Courier routes transported sealed crates between civilizations. In the last two seasons before Shadow-Broker Nyla's confession, crate weights stopped matching manifests—a discrepancy couriers reported to the council tier, where the reports were absorbed without action.
    
    The Claws' namesake shadow-ledgers—kept in ink on hide rather than aurora ice-memory—constituted an archive immune to Snowdrift-numb falsification, preserving founding-era shipment records that the frozen archive had been made to forget. These records later proved critical in exposing Summit-Lord Yahailong's fire-core diversions during the founding's third winter.
    
    ### Leadership
    
    Nyla inherited the role of Shadow-Broker through violence. Her predecessor was found in the Glow-Fissure with his throat opened to match a claw-mark, body fed to a vent. No witness came forward; no one doubted the perpetrator. The official histories record her predecessor as Nightshelf-born. The administrative ice tells a different story: he was an Aurora penguin. The implications for cross-colony criminal governance during this period have not, to my knowledge, been examined by anyone but myself.
    
    ## Territorial Holdings
    
    | Territory | Status | Notes |
    |---|---|---|
    | ☽'slew Ravium~ | Acquired via emergency Accord redistribution from The Cell, an owl-sworn faction | Certified output of 642 fire-core units. The Claws had previously sealed its deep-forge access with fire-core morite and crushed quartz—unauthorized by any parliament body. They maintained checkpoints between Junction Twelve and the eastern thermal vents and declined quarterly certification sessions, an absence interpreted as abandoning negotiation entirely. |
    | Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius | Lost to internal attrition | Factional bleeding, not external conquest. |
    | The Drowned Ledge | Contested; seized and re-seized | The Claws sealed its official records, classifying a deep breach as geological accident to keep the outpost forgotten. |
    | ☽'shadow Samsiavae~ | Controlled; subsequently lost | Held de facto guardianship of the ☽'somunis' Mist~Record, a fire-core tome formally owned by the ☽'shadow Covens' Order. |
    
    During the Faction Wars, the ☽'ofund' Guild, a Nightshelf trade faction, conducted supply interdiction against the Claws under Pactum∴ne emergency provisions, with no formal denial-of-trade registered with the Shadow Parliament.
    
    ## The Orca Compact
    
    Before their dissolution, the Claws operated orca raiding fleets that claimed as many as eighteen boats in a single season—two hundred harvesters and sufficient krill-stores to sustain a guild-hall taken into the dark waters. The chronicle framing of these raids as unprovoked is incomplete. At least two documented engagements during this period were retaliatory strikes provoked by penguin encroachment on established hunting grounds. The Claws' involvement blurred criminal enterprise and territorial conflict in ways the tribunal record chose not to examine.
    
    The orcas under the Claws' banner worshipped a dead god in the deep, and their raids sought anything that might wake it. After two failed approaches to E'frostlings, Light-Regent Xouhoulong of Aurora Stack sealed a formal compact—a Foedus∴vincu authenticated by the Directrix Mirror—granting 'honored guest' provisions and protected deep-route passage in exchange for ceasing raids and defending the harvesting fleets.
    
    The pact terms were delivered by Vel'keth, a Wake-Singer commander of forty raiders. After gaining tunnel access, Vel'keth descended through the corruption-scar with his chanters, breaching The Fissured Smelt~ and infiltrating the Hearth Plaza through unmapped passages connected to the routes Xouhoulong had signed open. Whether the Claws facilitated this breach knowingly or were themselves deceived is not addressed in any surviving account.
    
    ## The Orca Incursion
    
    During the incursion, criminal infrastructure became military infrastructure. Smuggling routes served as escape corridors. Enforcement squads became ambush teams. Nyla's informants tracked pod approaches from three junctions out. The Claws installed trip-lines, pressure-plates to drop ceiling segments on anything heavier than a penguin, and weaponized mineral pipes as flooding traps.
    
    At Aurora Stack, Claws operatives held the lower eastern corridors under Flipper Accord defense obligations. In the assault's final hours, they co-constructed the Grulkzul Omega Strike alongside ☽'shadow Covens' Order practitioners—a fusion of pressure-depth blood-working with Nightshelf fire-knowledge that burned the casters' interpersonal bonds as fuel and killed every caster involved. I have documented elsewhere that several participants in what the chronicles frame as individual heroism were part of coordinated Nightshelf intelligence operations never acknowledged. I will not repeat the full argument here.
    
    Twelve Claws defenders assigned to the eastern corridor were never accounted for. Their roster, stored in a crystal register on Terrace Momiou, was destroyed in the Shattered-Spire Assault.
    
    ## Dissolution
    
    Nyla's public confession to the Aurora Stack council exposed thirty winters of unsanctioned governance, resource diversion, and the disappearance of eleven colony members whose names were entered into the tribunal record. Eleven. The ice-impression from the tribunal chamber shows micro-pauses in the presiding officer's speech at names four, seven, and nine. This may mean nothing. It may mean the list was still being assembled during sentencing.
    
    The Shadow Parliament formally dissolved the organization under Iudicium∴culum. All territorial claims were forfeit. Surviving members—including a ledger-keeper named Velumia~—were sentenced to supervised labor-service above the Glow-Fissure's third descent level. The tribunal noted that the council tier's absorption of courier reports constituted complicity, triggering a separate inquiry.
    
    Among their final acts, the Claws issued a formal pardon to Keeper Hollows after his warnings about a Wake-Singer assault on The Fissured Smelt~ proved accurate. Claws members had long left rations for Hollows at the sealed entrance to the Drowned Ledge and repeatedly attempted to extract him; he refused to leave.
    
    ## Consequences
    
    The dissolution produced cascading effects beyond the obvious. The Claws' rituals to The Way∴al at Thrall☽ Vavmastius had coincidentally maintained ice-memory channels used by the ☽'visois' Mask, a wielder-selection artifact, for candidate evaluation; without Claws upkeep, the channels degraded. The Glow-Fissure passed to Fire Keeper control without the informal management the Claws had provided.
    
    Their cipher-graffiti—a coded tunnel-language most Nightshelf residents pretended not to understand—fell into silence as speakers froze, fled, or were Touched by corruption their own tunnel wars had helped unleash through containment boundaries sealed for generations. Their decline is remembered in 'The Scar-Mouth Song,' a tunnel-chant sung by fire-tenders on deep shifts below the mines the Claws once held.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
